---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site.js';
---
<PageLayout
  title="Editorial Method — How Our Milling Articles Are Written"
  description="Who writes the Haiyun Knowledge Centre, what the figures are based on, how the data is checked, and how corrections are handled."
>
  <section class="bg-steel-950">
    <div class="container-x py-14">
      <p class="eyebrow-light">Editorial</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-4xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-white">How these articles are written</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-steel-400">
        Fifty-two technical articles is a lot of claim-making. This page sets out where the
        numbers come from, who is accountable for them, and what to do when one is wrong.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-steel-500">Last updated: 8 November 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container-x max-w-3xl">
      <div class="prose-hy">
        <h2>Who writes this</h2>
        <p>
          The Knowledge Centre is written by the engineering desk at {site.legalName} — the
          same team that sizes flow sheets, specifies equipment and commissions plants. Articles
          are drafted by an engineer working on live projects, then reviewed by the technical
          director before publication. Nothing is published by a marketing function without
          engineering sign-off.
        </p>
        <p>
          We are an equipment manufacturer, not an independent research body. That is a real
          limitation and you should weigh it: our commercial interest is in plants that are
          specified properly, which is not the same as an interest in the largest possible
          quotation. Where our advice costs you less than the alternative, we say so.
        </p>

        <h2>What the figures are based on</h2>
        <p>Numbers on this site come from four places, in descending order of authority:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Commissioning and acceptance-test records</strong> from plants we have built
            — tonnage, extraction, ash, specific power, moisture accuracy. These are the source
            for the project case studies.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Factory test data</strong> for individual machines — capacity, power draw,
            dimensions, weight — measured on our own test floor before crating.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Standard milling practice</strong> for rules of thumb such as sifting area
            per tonne per day, passage counts per flow sheet, and tempering times. These are
            industry conventions, not proprietary findings, and they are stated as ranges
            because the underlying conditions vary.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Published technical literature</strong> — miller's handbooks, manufacturer
            technical bulletins, and standards bodies — for physical constants such as dust
            Kst, minimum ignition energy, and ash measurement temperatures.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Where a number is an estimate rather than a measurement, it is written as a range.
          Where a range would be misleading because the answer depends entirely on local
          conditions, we say what it depends on instead of inventing a midpoint.
        </p>

        <h2>Why the case studies are specific</h2>
        <p>
          The four project records report figures to one decimal place — 76.4% extraction,
          68.9 kWh/t, 12.28% blend protein. That precision is deliberate: it comes from the
          acceptance-test sheets and the plant historian, and rounding it to "about 76%" would
          hide the variation that matters to a buyer comparing two quotations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Client names are withheld at the request of the plant owners. Locations, tonnages,
          flow sheets and performance figures are unchanged. A plant built to the same design
          will not reproduce these numbers exactly — wheat, climate, shift pattern and operator
          skill all move the result, and the case study pages say so.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Technical content goes out of date, and we make mistakes. If you find a figure that
          contradicts another page, does not survive its own arithmetic, or conflicts with your
          operating experience, write to <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> with
          the page address and the discrepancy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Verified corrections are published with the article's updated date changed. We do not
          silently edit a number and leave the date alone — if you cite us, you should be able
          to tell whether the page changed since you read it.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
        <p>
          We do not publish sponsored content, we do not accept payment for product mentions,
          and we do not run affiliate links. There are no advertising networks on these pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also do not claim to be independent. Every article is written by the company that
          would like to sell you the plant. Read it as the considered view of a supplier who
          has to live with the consequences of their own advice — which is a different kind of
          accountability, and worth something, but not the same as a peer-reviewed journal.
        </p>

        <h2>How to use this material</h2>
        <p>
          Use it to ask better questions, to sanity-check a quotation, and to notice when two
          suppliers are quoting different things. Do not use it to size a plant without
          site-specific assessment — the{' '}
          <a href="/terms/">terms of use</a> explain why, and the
          <a href="/knowledge/">knowledge centre index</a> is the fastest way to find the
          article that covers your question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CTABand />
</PageLayout>
